# Just Write What I Tell You!
Tamil title: சொல்வதை எழுதேண்டா!

"Hey, Mandu! Finished eating? All right, sit down for a while. My hand is aching a little. I shall dictate; write it down properly, understand?"

"All right, Uncle! A letter?"

"What does it matter to you what it is? Just write what I tell you!"

"Ah, here! I shall bring the letter paper."

"Let me see how clever you are. Which letter paper are you bringing?"

"Why, the *Journalist* letter paper."

"The very name Mandu suits you! We must now write a letter to Diwan Bahadur Theerthagiri Mudaliar. That letter paper will not do. Go and bring the one headed 'Raghavachariar, President, Rama Bhakta Jana Sabha.'"

"The one printed with a picture of Hanuman?"

"That is the one, Mandu! Exactly that."

Uncle and nephew-in-law habitually spoke to each other in this playful fashion. I do not mean that this happened everywhere; it was the custom in Raghavachariar's own household.

"Why, girl! Our Rukku looks splendid today. If her husband sees her, he will faint!" Raghavachariar would say even to his own daughter Rukku. At times he would ask, "Why are you standing there rubbing your chin? Did Sundaram bite you?" Sundaram was Rukku's husband. He too would joke with his father-in-law: "I do not understand these games Father-in-law plays!"

"Mandu" was another nephew-in-law, though related only according to kinship terminology. Raghavachariar had not made the relationship firm by giving him a daughter, because he had no other daughter to give. He had summoned Varadan with the promise of finding him a job. Varadan was the one who bore the title "Mandu."

Mandu returned with the letter paper and sat down to write. The Iyer began his dictation.

"To the illustrious Diwan Bahadur Theerthagiri Mudaliar..."

Mandu wrote rapidly and repeated, "...Mudaliar."

"Hey! Did you put 'Sri Rama Jayam' at the top?" Raghavachariar asked.

"No. You did not tell me to," said Mandu.

"Get along! Must I tell you even that?" Raghavachariar replied.

Mandu added it and asked him to continue.

"May every blessing attend Mudaliar and may all his desires be fulfilled," Raghavachariar dictated.

As Mandu wrote, Raghavachariar described the recipient's desires: "Do you know what he desires? He wants to obtain a two-crore fortune. He wants the entire nation to tremble at the words 'Diwan Bahadur Mudaliar.' He wants to buy the Governor's bungalow. There will be desires of every such kind."

Mandu stopped writing. "Uncle, is he so greedy? Why then do you bless such a man by wishing that every one of his desires be fulfilled?"

"Will you write what I tell you or not? Do all his desires become reality merely because I say so? He gains nothing from it, and what do we lose by using the phrase? Send him such words and the fellow will grin broadly. What else? Write!" Mandu wrote.

Raghavachariar resumed: "However much hardship I suffer, I have no habit of telling others about it..."

Hearing this, Mandu did not write. He laughed.

"Uncle, what a claim! From the woman who sweeps the doorstep to every person who comes and goes, you do nothing but relate your troubles. Yet you ask me to write that you never tell anyone else about them!"

He was Mandu, after all, and dared question his uncle. Did he know that his uncle's special methods had borne fruit as diamond earrings in his aunt's ears, a chain around Rukku's neck and a wristwatch on the elder son-in-law's hand? How could Raghavachariar explain every matter to him? He glared. Mandu wrote.

"You know that my son Panju is studying in the B.A. class," Raghavachariar dictated. Mandu wrote without comment.

"Why do you have no question about this? You ought to ask how I can call him Panju when he signs himself R. P. Nathan," Raghavachariar teased.

The son's name was Panchanathan, and his father shortened it affectionately to Panju. As a fashionable abbreviation, however, the young man habitually wrote his name as R. P. Nathan. Mandu dismissed the matter as nothing remarkable.

"Continue, Uncle," he said.

"Only Sri Ramachandra Murti himself knows the difficulty I face in meeting Panju's college expenses," said Raghavachariar.

Mandu grunted acknowledgment as he wrote.

"This month in particular, the anniversary of the Rama Bhakta Jana Sabha has swallowed an enormous sum," the uncle continued.

Mandu wrote. He knew exactly where in the house one could find the grapes, almonds, cashews, dhoti, silver plate and many other articles acquired under cover of that anniversary celebration. What could he do with such knowledge? He had to write whatever Raghavachariar told him.

"Therefore, you must show me great kindness and give the boy fifty rupees," said Raghavachariar.

"Fifty?" Mandu asked once.

He doubted whether Panchanathan needed any money at all. The young man had even sent his father by money order the twenty rupees he received for acting in a radio play. In their delight, the family had offered the Lord sweet akkāravadisal.

That being so, Mandu wondered why Panju needed fifty rupees.

"Yes, fifty, fool! Would one ask a man of such stature for merely five or ten? Are we drivers employed in his household?" Raghavachariar explained.

"At this moment, those fifty rupees would be equal to five hundred for me," he added.

Mandu did not understand this either. Raghavachariar's elder brother was a local priest of the old school. At ceremonies he prompted the recipient to say that however much a household gave, even when it was little, it should be regarded as a great sum. Was not Raghavachariar himself a priest of the modern school?

"Your charitable disposition and generous nature..." Raghavachariar dictated. Mandu wrote it down.

Without finishing the sentence, Raghavachariar paused to think.

"Why have you stopped, Uncle? I have written 'generous nature,'" said Mandu.

"Wait. The more praise we heap on him, the better. It will especially delight that fellow. 'Charitable disposition' and 'generous nature' are not enough. We should add something else. What shall we put? You suggest something."

"Shall we write 'devotion to Brahmins'?" Mandu proposed.

"Madman! We must not use that phrase at all. These days people everywhere mock the very expression 'devotion to Brahmins.' Even this man helps us when occasion requires, but while speaking with others he habitually abuses our entire community as 'these Brahmin fellows.' Once he spoke that way right in front of me. Just see his arrogance," said Raghavachariar, describing the Diwan Bahadur's character.

"In front of you? Did you remain silent, Uncle?" asked Mandu.

"Should I have picked a quarrel with him instead of remaining silent? Which matters more, getting the business done or displaying courage? I listened without saying anything. Then he suspected that I might take offence because I was there. He looked at me and asked with a smile, 'Why, Sami? Are you angry that I am criticizing our community?'

"I delivered a fine reply: 'Not at all, Mudaliar! You spoke only in accordance with the ways of the world.' The fellow was immensely satisfied. 'Your case is different, Sami. I was not criticizing Brahmins who possess your character and heart. I meant only the others,' he explained.

"That day he gave ten rupees more than usual toward the expenses of the coronation ceremony at which our Narasimhachariar recited the *Ramayana* here. That is the sort of man the Diwan Bahadur is," Raghavachariar concluded.

After a little more thought, he added the phrase "blessed with the Lord's grace" to "charitable disposition" and "generous nature."

He completed the sentence: "Nothing more need be said to one such as yourself, who is blessed with the Lord's grace."

The letter was finished, and Mandu yawned.

"While you are here, write one more letter, to Panju," said Raghavachariar.

Mandu sat down again.

"Take the *Journalist* letter paper," said his uncle, and began:

"Blessings to long-lived Panchanathan. By this same post today I am asking the Diwan Bahadur to give you fifty rupees urgently. He will give it. Collect it, take five or ten rupees if you need anything for your expenses, and with the rest immediately buy and send your elder sister a six-yard Bengal silk sari in orange. I hear it is available in China Bazaar.

"The District Collector is coming here next Thursday, apparently to raise money for the army. In connection with it, our Rukku, Rajam from the house opposite and Shanbagam, the wife of the deaf gentleman in the corner house, are all going to perform a Bengal dance. The sari is therefore urgent.

"Theerthagiri Mudaliar is rather hot-tempered and may snap at you. Pay no attention and wait a little; he will give you the money. Speak to him tactfully. He is enormously fond of flattery, a great sensualist too. Even so, he will not refuse to give us money. He is the patron of our Rama Bhakta Jana Sabha. I have made him believe that this is the very reason he received the title of Diwan Bahadur. If an opportunity arises, flatter him in the same fashion. Obtain the money somehow. Do not behave foolishly and return without it."

This was a letter from a father to his son. Mandu wrote it without difficulty and without asking a single awkward question, for he had now understood Raghavachariar's method.

"Oh! I forgot. We must add one more line to Panju's letter," said Raghavachariar.

"Tell me. There is room," said Mandu.

"When you go to Theerthagiri's house, wrap a little dried loose flower and turmeric powder in a paper packet, take it with you, and tell him that your father sent it from the village." With this instruction, he completed the letter.

Raghavachariar's mail set out on its journey. Thinking of the Bengal silk sari, Rukku began practising her steps. They themselves resembled a dance of a novel kind.

The letters arrived, but the letter intended for Mudaliar reached Panju, while the one meant for Panju reached Mudaliar. Was a Bengal silk sari likely to come now?

Diwan Bahadur Theerthagiri Mudaliar abused Raghavachariar as "an unworthy wretch, an ingrate and a deceitful schemer," declared, "Just watch what I do to him," and threatened to kick Panju as well.

Only after Panju wrote his father a detailed letter did Raghavachariar understand the disaster Mandu had caused. Panju described the Mudaliar's abuse and threats and explained that, through Mandu's mistake, the letter addressed to him had been placed in the envelope bearing Mudaliar's address, while the letter written to Mudaliar had been placed in Panju's envelope.

"I only wrote what you told me," Mandu muttered.

Striking his own head, Raghavachariar said, "You are not Mandu, my boy. I am Mandu. I should blame myself for enlisting you in this business."

He sank into thought for a while. Then he called, "Hey, Mandu, come here! Even after all this, I have no one else to turn to. Go and bring the *Journalist* letter paper."

In a voice heavy with sorrow, Raghavachariar dictated. Mandu silently wrote:

### Rama Bhakta Jana Sabha

Members of this town's Sri Rama Bhakta Jana Sabha lodged a complaint with its president, Sriman Raghavachariar, alleging that Diwan Bahadur Theerthagiri Mudaliar, who had served as patron of the said Sabha, had been conducting himself contrary to religious propriety. The president examined the matter and, finding grounds for the complaint, removed the said Theerthagiri Mudaliar from his position as patron of the Sabha.

The most important among the several charges made against him is that he has been maintaining an improper relationship with a Brahmin widow.

"To whom shall I send it, Uncle? Tell me clearly," Mandu asked fearfully.

"Make ten copies and send them to every newspaper: *Swadesamitran*, *The Hindu*, *Ananda Vikatan*, *Mani*, and the rest," Raghavachariar ordered. Mandu carried out the instruction.

Then, again in a frightened voice, he asked Raghavachariar, "Uncle, do not be angry, but tell me just this. Who is that Brahmin widow?"

"Our Kamali," said Raghavachariar.

Kamali was his wife's cousin.

24 February 1946

---NOTES---
The title and dialogue preserve the comic force of colloquial Brahmin Tamil. “Mandu” is retained as the character's nickname while its sense of “fool” or “dullard” is made clear by context. The story's allegations and descriptions of named characters are translated as fictional source content and are not presented as independently verified claims.
